### Step 2: Understand the JavaScript concepts

| Concept                  | Simple explanation                            |
| ------------------------ | --------------------------------------------- |
| document                 | Represents the current HTML page              |
| document.documentElement | Refers to the root <html> element             |
| DOMContentLoaded         | Fires after the HTML document has been parsed |
| getAttribute()           | Reads an HTML attribute                       |
| setAttribute()           | Adds or updates an HTML attribute             |
| localStorage.getItem()   | Reads a saved browser value                   |
| localStorage.setItem()   | Saves a browser value                         |
| addEventListener()       | Registers code to run when an event occurs    |

For example:

```javascript
document.documentElement.setAttribute(
    "data-theme",
    "dark"
);
```

This changes the root HTML element's theme attribute to `dark`. Our CSS then applies the dark theme variables.

### Section 9 — Load the saved theme

Use this line to retrieve the user's previous choice:

```javascript
const savedTheme = localStorage.getItem("theme");
```

There are two main possibilities:

* The user previously selected Dark Mode, so `savedTheme` is `"dark"`.

* The user has never selected a theme, so `savedTheme` is `null`.

We should validate the saved value rather than blindly trusting it:

```javascript
const isValidTheme =
    savedTheme === "light" || savedTheme === "dark";

const initialTheme = isValidTheme
    ? savedTheme
    : "light";
```

This ensures the application uses only recognized values. If storage is empty or contains an invalid value, Light Mode is the fallback.

### Section 10 — Save the selected theme

Use `localStorage.setItem()`:

```javascript
localStorage.setItem("theme", "dark");
```

This stores a string under the key `theme`.

Local Storage generally persists across refreshes and browser restarts, until the user clears site data or the browser removes the data. It is associated with the site's origin, which includes its scheme, host, and port.

For example, `https://localhost:7001` and `https://example.com` have separate storage areas.

Unlike server session storage, Local Storage does not automatically expire after a few minutes. That makes it suitable for a long-term theme preference.

Important: Browser storage may be unavailable in some restricted environments. Accessing it can throw an exception, so our final script will handle that gracefully.

### Section 11 — Toggle the theme

The theme toggle performs five operations:

* Reads the current theme.

* Determines the opposite theme.

* Updates the root HTML attribute.

* Saves the new value to Local Storage.

* Updates the icon and accessible button state.

The core logic looks like this:

```javascript
const currentTheme =
    document.documentElement.getAttribute("data-theme");

const nextTheme =
    currentTheme === "dark" ? "light" : "dark";

document.documentElement.setAttribute(
    "data-theme",
    nextTheme
);

localStorage.setItem("theme", nextTheme);
```

The conditional expression `condition ? valueA : valueB` is called a ternary operator. It means:

* If the current theme is dark, switch to light.

* Otherwise, switch to dark.

We will combine this logic with storage error handling and icon updates in the complete script.

### Section 12 — Update the moon and sun icons

The icon should reflect the action the user can take.

| Current theme | Icon shown | Button text |
| ------------- | ---------- | ----------- |
| Light         | Moon       | Dark Mode   |
| Dark          | Sun        | Light Mode  |

This makes the toggle easier to understand without requiring the user to guess what it does.

JavaScript can update the icons using:

```javascript
moonIcon.hidden = theme === "dark";
sunIcon.hidden = theme !== "dark";
```

It can also update the accessible state:

```javascript
button.setAttribute(
    "aria-pressed",
    String(theme === "dark")
);
```

We will put these operations inside a reusable function named `updateThemeUI()`.

### Section 13 — Connect CSS and JavaScript

Open `Views/Shared/_Layout.cshtml`.

### Step 1: Reference the stylesheet in `<head>`

Add this after Bootstrap's CSS and after `site.css`:

```html
<link rel="stylesheet" href="~/css/theme.css" asp-append-version="true" />
```

Why?

* `rel="stylesheet"` tells the browser this file contains CSS.

* `~/css/theme.css` points to the file under `wwwroot`.

* `asp-append-version="true"` adds a version query string based on the file contents, helping browsers fetch updated CSS after changes.

Place `theme.css` after `site.css` so your theme rules can override the default site styles where their selectors have equal specificity.

### Step 2: Reference the JavaScript before `</body>`

Add:

```html
<script src="~/js/theme.js" asp-append-version="true"></script>
```

Place it after the page content and before the closing `</body>` tag.

Loading the script at the end of the body means most of the HTML elements have already been parsed when the script runs. We will still use `DOMContentLoaded` so initialization is safe and predictable.

### Step 3: Why not put the code directly into `_Layout.cshtml`?

A separate JavaScript file is easier to maintain, debug, and reuse. The layout should contain the shared page structure, while `theme.js` should contain theme behavior.

One further improvement is to load the saved theme as early as possible in the document head to reduce a flash of the wrong theme on page refresh. We will cover that production-oriented improvement in the final solution.

