### Section 16 — Best practices for a production-oriented implementation

### 1\. Use CSS variables

Avoid duplicating a complete set of styles for every theme. Define the colors once and use `var()` throughout your CSS.

### 2\. Keep JavaScript separate

Store the theme logic in `wwwroot/js/theme.js`. Avoid putting the entire implementation into a Razor view.

### 3\. Use transitions carefully

A short transition such as `0.2s ease` helps the interface feel smooth. Avoid excessive animations, especially for users who prefer reduced motion.

### 4\. Respect Bootstrap

Bootstrap's default components have their own styles. Use Bootstrap for structure and responsive layout, and custom CSS variables for your application's theme.

For Bootstrap 5.3, you can also set `data-bs-theme="dark"` or `data-bs-theme="light"` to use Bootstrap's built-in color-mode support. Our implementation will synchronize that attribute with `data-theme`.

### 5\. Consider accessibility

* Keep sufficient contrast between text and backgrounds.

* Use a real `<button>` instead of a clickable `<span>`.

* Maintain keyboard focus indicators.

* Update accessible labels when the action changes.

* Do not rely on color alone to communicate status.

### 6\. Prevent a flash of the wrong theme

If you only read Local Storage when `theme.js` loads at the end of the page, the browser may briefly render Light Mode before switching to Dark Mode.

A small, guarded initialization script in the document head can set the theme before the stylesheet renders. This is included in the final solution below.

### 7\. Plan for IIS hosting

When you eventually publish the application to IIS:

* Keep `theme.css` and `theme.js` under `wwwroot`.

* Use `asp-append-version="true"` to reduce stale CSS/JavaScript cache issues.

* Test the deployed HTTPS URL separately from `localhost`.

* Remember that Local Storage is specific to the deployed origin.

* Ensure static files are served by the ASP.NET Core application and that IIS hosting is correctly configured.

